//! core/net/websocket — dialling somebody else's WebSocket.
//!
//! `core/net/server` is the accepting half. This is the other half — a program
//! that *dials* a socket somebody else is holding. The hooks are the same three,
//! the state threads the same way, and `Socket`, `Message` and `CloseReason` are
//! that module's own types re-exported here.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Sockets, Stdout, WebSocketClient };
//! from "core/host" import * as host;
//! from "core/io" import * as io;
//! from "core/net/websocket" import * as websocket;
//!
//! export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Stdout: host.stdout,
//!         Sockets: host.sockets,
//!         WebSocketClient: host.websocketClient,
//!     };
//!     websocket
//!         .connect(ctx, websocket.Client {
//!             url: "ws://127.0.0.1:3000/socket",
//!             onOpen: fn(c, socket, _response) => {
//!                 let _sent = socket.send(c, .Text("hello"));
//!                 0
//!             },
//!             onMessage: fn(c, socket, count, _message) => {
//!                 let _closed = socket.close(c, .Normal);
//!                 count + 1
//!             },
//!             onClose: fn(c, _socket, count, _reason) => {
//!                 let _said = io.println(c, "got ${count} frames").ignore();
//!                 count
//!             },
//!         })
//!         .map(fn(_ended) => ())
//!         .mapErr(websocket.errorText)
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## `connect` returns when the socket closes
//!
//! One call is the socket's whole life: dial, run `onOpen`, run `onMessage` for
//! each message, and run `onClose` when the far side goes or this end closes.
//! The answer is the reason it ended **and the state `onClose` answered**, so
//! what a socket learned leaves with it.
//!
//! `.Err` means the socket was never opened — a URL this platform cannot dial, a
//! machine that refused the connection, a server that did not answer `101`. Once
//! a socket exists every ending is an `.Ok` carrying a reason.
//!
//! **Reconnecting is a loop around it.** There is no reconnect knob, no backoff
//! setting and no retry count — and because the state comes back out, the next
//! dial starts from what the last one learned. Here that is a session-resume
//! token: it arrives in a frame, lands in the state, and goes back on the wire
//! in the next socket's `onOpen`.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Allocator, Clock, Sockets, WebSocketClient };
//! from "core/net/websocket" import * as websocket;
//! from "core/net/websocket" import { Client };
//! from "core/str" import * as str;
//! from "core/time" import * as time;
//!
//! fn resuming<C: Allocator + Sockets + WebSocketClient>(token: Str): Client<C, Str> {
//!     Client {
//!         url: "ws://127.0.0.1:3000/socket",
//!         onOpen: fn(c, socket, _response) => {
//!             let _sent = socket.send(c, .Text(str.format(c, "resume ${token}")));
//!             token
//!         },
//!         onMessage: fn(_c, _socket, sofar, message) => {
//!             match (message) {
//!                 .Text(text) => text,
//!                 .Binary(_data) => sofar,
//!             }
//!         },
//!         onClose: fn(_c, _socket, sofar, _reason) => sofar,
//!     }
//! }
//!
//! export fn following<C: Allocator + Clock + Sockets + WebSocketClient>(
//!     ctx: C,
//!     token: Str,
//!     tries: Int,
//! ): Str {
//!     match (websocket.connect(ctx, resuming(token))) {
//!         .Ok(ended) => {
//!             if (tries <= 0) {
//!                 ended.1
//!             } else {
//!                 let _slept = time.sleep(ctx, time.milliseconds(1000));
//!                 following(ctx, ended.1, tries - 1)
//!             }
//!         },
//!         .Err(_unreachable) => token,
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## What the hooks see
//!
//! `onOpen` is handed the `Response` that opened the socket, where
//! `core/net/server`'s is handed the `Request` that asked for one. It is where a
//! negotiated subprotocol arrives.
//!
//! Everything else reads the same as a server's hooks. `onOpen` answers the
//! socket's first state, every later hook is handed the current one, and
//! `onMessage` answers the next. `onClose` runs once, with the last state, and
//! answers the state `connect` hands back; the socket is already closed by
//! then, so anything sent from there is dropped.
//! `onMessage` sees `.Text` and `.Binary` and nothing else — a ping and a pong
//! are the platform keeping the connection alive.
//!
//! **Pushing needs `Sockets` and nothing else.** A `Socket` here is the same
//! inert integer a server's is, so it can go to an actor and be pushed on from
//! there. That is why `connect`'s bound is `WebSocketClient + Sockets`: the
//! first dials and the second writes.
//!
//! ## Headers and subprotocols are the URL's business
//!
//! `Client` has a `url` and no header list, because a browser's `WebSocket`
//! cannot send request headers at all. Put a token or a subprotocol in the query
//! string, and read what you were given back off the response in `onOpen`.
//!
//! ## On a page, and in a worker
//!
//! `connect` follows `ui.mount`: it suspends without holding the event loop, so
//! a page that mounted an interface and then dialled a socket goes on rendering
//! while the socket is idle. A pushed frame wakes it exactly as a click does. A
//! worker dials the same way while it is answering a request.
//!
//! On `LINUX` and `MACOS` this runtime writes the handshake and checks every
//! clause of the answer. Everywhere else the platform's own `WebSocket` does, so
//! how strictly a bad `101` is refused is that engine's decision, and
//! `Connected.headers` carries what the engine exposes — the negotiated
//! subprotocol and extensions — rather than the head the server sent.
//!
//! **The close code is that engine's decision too.** A page reads a `close`
//! event rather than a close frame, so `onClose`'s `CloseReason` is whatever the
//! engine made of it: `node` reports the code the far side sent and `bun`
//! reports `.Abnormal` for every close it did not start. And a close a page
//! *sends* goes out as 1000 unless it is 1000 or in 3000–4999, which is
//! `Sockets.socketClose`'s note. Put anything a program has to act on in a
//! message, not in a code.

from "core/effect" import {
    Connected, Received, Response, ServeError, Sockets, WebSocketClient,
};
from "core/net/server" import { CloseReason, Message, Socket };

from "core/effect" export { Connected, Response, ServeError };

from "core/net/server" export { CloseReason, errorText, Message, Socket };

/// A client, whole: where to dial, and the three hooks over the socket it gets.
///
/// `C` is the caller's own context and every hook is handed it, so a hook may do
/// anything the context it was written beside can do. `S` is **the state this
/// socket carries**: `onOpen` answers the first one, every later hook is handed
/// the current one and answers the next, and `onClose`'s answer is `connect`'s.
/// `core/net/server`'s `WebSocket` says the same thing about the same three
/// hooks.
export struct Client<C, S> {
    /// Where to dial. `ws://…` or `wss://…`; anything else is
    /// `.Err(.Unsupported)` from `connect`.
    ///
    /// **The whole of the request.** A subprotocol and a token go in the query
    /// string, because a page cannot send a header on a WebSocket handshake.
    export url: Str,
    /// The socket is open. Answers its first state.
    ///
    /// The `Response` is the `101` the server answered — its status and its
    /// header fields — and it is where a negotiated subprotocol arrives.
    export onOpen: fn(C, Socket, Response) => S,
    /// A message arrived. Answers the state the next one will see.
    export onMessage: fn(C, Socket, S, Message) => S,
    /// The socket is gone. Runs once, with the last state, and answers the
    /// state `connect` hands back.
    ///
    /// **The socket is already closed when this runs**, so anything sent from
    /// here is dropped. Use it to undo what `onOpen` did, and to say what this
    /// socket leaves behind — a resume token the next dial sends back, say.
    export onClose: fn(C, Socket, S, CloseReason) => S,
}

/// Dial, run the hooks, and answer the reason the socket ended beside the state
/// it ended holding.
///
/// One call and one socket. It returns when the socket closes, so a program that
/// wants a second one calls it again — and the `S` in the answer is what the
/// second one starts from. The module header writes that loop out.
///
/// `.Err` is a socket that never opened, and there is no state in it because no
/// hook ran. Everything after the handshake is `.Ok`: `.Ok((.Normal, s))` is a
/// clean goodbye and `.Ok((.Abnormal, s))` is a connection that dropped.
///
/// **`Sockets` is in the bound because the hooks push.** `connect` itself never
/// calls a `Sockets` method — `socket.send` and `socket.close` are the hooks' —
/// but the hooks are handed this context, so the authority has to be in it.
export fn connect<C: WebSocketClient + Sockets, S>(
    ctx: C,
    client: Client<C, S>,
): Result<(CloseReason, S), ServeError> {
    match (ctx.connectSocket(client.url)) {
        .Err(e) => .Err(e),
        .Ok(connected) => .Ok(opened(ctx, client, connected)),
    }
}

/// A socket, from the hook that opens it to the hook that closes it. The
/// `Response` is built here, out of the three fields `Connected` carries flat.
fn opened<C: WebSocketClient, S>(
    ctx: C,
    client: Client<C, S>,
    connected: Connected,
): (CloseReason, S) {
    let socket = Socket(connected.socket);
    let open = client.onOpen;
    let state = open(ctx, socket, Response {
        status: connected.status,
        headers: connected.headers,
        body: connected.body,
    });
    pumping(ctx, client, socket, state)
}

/// One message, then the next, then the close.
///
/// A self tail call, so a socket that carries a million messages costs one
/// frame.
///
/// **`.Err` is a close too.** A socket that is already gone when this asks
/// reaches `onClose` as `.Abnormal` rather than getting dropped.
fn pumping<C: WebSocketClient, S>(
    ctx: C,
    client: Client<C, S>,
    socket: Socket,
    state: S,
): (CloseReason, S) {
    match (ctx.connectReceive(socket.0)) {
        .Err(_gone) => closed(ctx, client, socket, state, .Abnormal),
        .Ok(received) => stepped(ctx, client, socket, state, received),
    }
}

/// One thing that arrived, as the hook it runs.
fn stepped<C: WebSocketClient, S>(
    ctx: C,
    client: Client<C, S>,
    socket: Socket,
    state: S,
    received: Received,
): (CloseReason, S) {
    match (received.frame) {
        .Closed => closed(ctx, client, socket, state, reasonOf(received.code)),
        .Text => {
            let step = client.onMessage;
            pumping(ctx, client, socket, step(ctx, socket, state, .Text(received.text)))
        },
        .Binary => {
            let step = client.onMessage;
            pumping(
                ctx,
                client,
                socket,
                step(ctx, socket, state, .Binary(received.data)),
            )
        },
    }
}

/// The close hook, run once, with the state the last message left. Its answer
/// and the reason are what `connect` answers.
fn closed<C, S>(
    ctx: C,
    client: Client<C, S>,
    socket: Socket,
    state: S,
    reason: CloseReason,
): (CloseReason, S) {
    let close = client.onClose;
    (reason, close(ctx, socket, state, reason))
}

/// The number a socket closed with, as the reason a program reads.
///
/// The same mapping `core/net/server` applies to a server's sockets, private in
/// both modules so a program never sees the numbers.
///
/// A negative code is the platform's own and never the far side's — wire codes
/// are unsigned — so nothing a server can send reaches the first arm. Every
/// number this enum does not name is `.Abnormal`, including 1005 and 1006.
fn reasonOf(code: Int): CloseReason {
    if (code < 0) {
        .Overflow
    } else {
        match (code) {
            1000 => .Normal,
            1001 => .GoingAway,
            1002 => .ProtocolError,
            1003 => .Unsupported,
            1008 => .Policy,
            1009 => .TooLarge,
            1011 => .InternalError,
            _ => .Abnormal,
        }
    }
}
